===Etymology 1===
From Late {{inh|en|enm|carpe|t=the common carp (''Cyprinus carpio'')}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=carpe|pos=n|id=MED6851}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|carpe}}, from {{der|en|LL.|carpa}}, possibly from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*karpo}} (possibly due to the introduction from the fish from the Danube into England in the 14th century;<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> whence {{cog|gml|karpe}} and {{cog|goh|charpho}}, {{m|goh|karpho}}); further etymology unknown.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=7595110816|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=carp{{sup|1}}|pos=n}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|carp|s}}

# {{senseid|en|fish}} Any of various [[freshwater#Adjective|freshwater]] [[fish#Noun|fish]] of the [[family#Noun|family]] [[Cyprinidae]]; specifically the [[common carp]], ''[[Cyprinus carpio]]''.
#* {{RQ:Walton Compleat Angler|chapter=VIII|pages=161–162|pageref=161|passage=The '''''Carp''''' is a ſtately, a good, and a ſubtle fiſh, a fiſh that hath not (as it is ſaid) been long in ''England'', but ſaid to be by one Mr. ''Maſcall'' (a Gentleman then living at ''Plumſted'' in ''Suſſex'') brought into this Nation: {{...}} '''''Carps''''' and ''Loches'' are obſerved to breed ſeveral months in one year, vvhich moſt other fiſh do not, and it is the rather believed, becauſe you ſhall ſcarce or never take a ''Male '''Carp''''' vvithout a ''Melt'', or a ''Female'' vvithout a ''Roe'' or ''Spavvn''; {{...}}}}

