===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kerp-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|carpen}}, {{m|enm|karpe|t=to chat, converse, talk; to chatter, gossip; to ask; to cry out, wail; to find fault, carp; to relate, tell; to recite; to sing}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=carpen|pos=v|id=MED6853}}</ref> and then partly:<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''v.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=5214441308|date=September 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=carp{{sup|2}}|pos=v}}</ref>

* from {{der|en|non|karpa|t=to boast, brag; to dispute, quarrel}}, further etymology unknown; and
* from, or influenced by, {{der|en|la|carpere}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|carpō|t=to harvest, pick, pluck; to criticize, revile, slander, carp at}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kerp-|t=to harvest, pluck}}.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=1|id=6051772929|date=September 2023}}</ref> ({{cog|enm|carp}}, {{m|enm|karp|t=conversation, discourse, talking; spoken or written message or statement; meaning; news; poem; song; story}}, from {{cog|non|karp|t=bragging}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|pos=n|id=MED6849}}</ref> did not survive into modern English.)

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To [[complain]] or [[criticize]] about a [[fault#Noun|fault]], especially for [[frivolous]] or [[petty#Adjective|petty]] [[reason#Noun|reasons]]; to [[cavil#Verb|cavil]].
##* {{RQ:Calvin Norton Institution|book=1|chapter=That there is Taught in the Scriptures One Essence of God from the Very Creation, which Essence Conteineth in It Thre Persons|folio=34|passage=Here agayne Seruetto '''carpeth''', y&#877; God did beare the perſon of an Angel. As thoughe the Prophete did not confirme that whiche [[w:Moses|Moſes]] had ſaied: why doeſt thou aſke me of my name?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Christopher Marlowe|C[hristoper] M&#91;arlowe&#93;]]|chapter=Elegia. 15. Ad inuidos, qaod fama poetarum sit perennis.|title=[[w:Amores (Ovid)|Ouids Elegies: Three Bookes.{{nb...|by C. M. Epigrames by I. D.}}]]|location=Middlebourgh &#91;''i.e.'', London|publisher={{...|Printed by}}[[w:Thomas Cotes|Thomas Cotes]]?&#93;|year=c. '''1580s''' (date written)|year_published=c. 1640|section=signature &#91;B6&#93;, verso|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_ouids-elegies-three-boo_ovidius-naso-publius/page/n27/mode/1up|oclc=65180432|passage=Enuie vvhy '''carpeſt''' thou my time is ſpent ſo ill, / And termſt my vvorkes fruites of an idle quill.}}
##* {{RQ:Barrow Evil-Speaking|part=2|pages=58–59|pageref=59|passage=[I]f vve ſhould never cenſure vvithout great Reaſon or neceſſity, hovv ſeldom ſhould vve do it? {{...}} [D]o vve not eagerly ſearch after, and greedily embrace all occaſions to do it? Is it not a pleaſant entertainment to us, to be '''carping''' and cavelling at any Body vve meet, at any thing vve ſee done?}}
##* {{RQ:Burns Poems|edition=2nd|volume=II|poem=JL2|page=95|passage=My vvorthy friend, ne'er grudge an' '''carp''', / Tho' Fortune uſe you hard an' ſharp; / Come, kittle up your ''moorland harp'' / VVi' gleeſome touch!}}
##* {{RQ:Arnold Mixed Essays|page=207|passage=[[w:Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon|[Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of] Clarendon]]'s touch, where in his memoirs he speaks of Falkland, is simpler than in the ''History''. But we will not '''carp''' at this great writer and faithful friend.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[speak#Verb|speak]], to [[talk#Verb|talk]]; also, to talk about a [[subject#Noun|subject]] in [[speech#Noun|speech]] or [[writing#Noun|writing]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To talk much but to little [[purpose#Noun|purpose]]; to [[chatter#Verb|chatter]], to [[prattle#Verb|prattle]].
##: {{synonyms|en|blabber|prate|Thesaurus:prattle}}
##* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Colyn Cloute|lines=548–552|page=332|passage=And some of them barke, / Clatter and '''carpe''' / Of that heresy arte / Called Wicleuista, / The deuelysshe dogmatista; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Surrey et al Songes|poem=A Praise of Maistresse R.|folio=84|verso=1|passage=And therwithall came curiouſneſſe and '''carped''' out of frame. / The audience laught to heare the ſtrife as they beheld the ſame.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=T[homas] Doubleday|title=Babington. A Tragedy|location=Edinburgh|publisher={{w|William Blackwood}}; London: [[w:Thomas Cadell (publisher)|T[homas] Cadell]],{{nb...|Strand, London.}}|year=1825|section=Act II, scene iii|page=54|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mtsGAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA54|oclc=1167601583|passage=Psha! thou '''carpest''' and '''carpest''', and yet tell'st nought; in a word, What say'st thou to him?}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} Of a [[bird#Noun|bird]]: to [[sing#Verb|sing]]; of a [[person#Noun|person]] (such as a [[minstrel#Noun|minstrel]]): to sing or [[recite]].
##* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Comely Coystrowne|lines=12–14|page=15|passage=Hys hart is to hy to haue any hap; / But for in his gamut '''carp''' that he can, / Lo, Jak wold be a jentylman!}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Minstrelsy|volume=I|poem=The Lochmaben Harper|page=67|passage=Then aye he harped, and aye he '''carped''', / Till a' the Lordlings footed the floor; / But an' the music was sae sweet, / The groom had nae mind o' the stable door.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}}
## To [[say#Verb|say]] or [[tell#Verb|tell]] (something).
## To [[find#Verb|find]] fault with (someone or something); to [[censure#Verb|censure]], to criticize.
##: {{synonyms|en|reprehend|reprove|take exception}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Colin Clout|chapter=Epistle Dedicatorie|sig=A2|page=1|passage=[W]ith your good countenance protect against the malice of euill mouthes, vvhich are alvvaies vvide open to '''carpe''' at and miſconstrue my ſimple meaning.}}
##* {{RQ:Camden Remaines|chapter=Grave Speeches, and Wittie Apothegmes of Woorthie Personages of This Realme in Former Times|page=177|passage=Albeit I doe knovve they [the speeches] vvill lie open to the cenſure of the youth of our time, vvho for the moſt part, are ſo over-gulled vvith ſelf-liking, that they are more then giddy in admiring themſelves, and '''carping''' vvhatſoever hath beene done or ſaide heeretofore, Nevertheleſſe I hope that all are not of one humour, and doubt not, but that there is diverſitie of taſtes, as vvas among ''[[w:Horace|Horaces]]'' gueſts; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Don Sebastian|scene=i|page=104|passage=[W]hen I ſpoke, / My honeſt homely vvords vvere '''carp'd''', and cenſur'd, / For vvant of Courtly ſtile: {{...}}}}

